.\" Manpage for osrep — Omega SREP
.\" View locally with:  man -l man/osrep.1
.TH OSREP 1 "September 2026" "Omega SREP 2.1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
osrep \- huge-dictionary LZ77 preprocessor (Omega lineage)

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B osrep
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB-m\fIN\fR \fIINFILE\fR \fIOUTFILE\fR
.br
.B osrep
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB-d\fR \fIARCHIVE\fR \fIOUTFILE\fR
.br
.B osrep
\fB-i\fR \fIARCHIVE\fR
.br
.B osrep
\fB--verify\fR \fIARCHIVE\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B osrep
is a content-defined-chunking and large-dictionary LZ77 preprocessor.
It scans an input stream for long-range duplicate byte sequences and emits a
small stream of unmatched bytes plus a list of (offset, length) match
records. The output is intended as input to a secondary entropy coder
(LZMA, ZPAQ, brotli, etc.) in a FreeArc-style pipeline; on its own it
typically compresses 30%–60% depending on input redundancy.

Omega SREP is a fork of upstream SREP 3.93a beta (Bulat Ziganshin, October
2014), extending it with a native FA-style dedup pre-pass
.RB ( -dup ),
deterministic seeding for reproducible archives
.RB ( --seed )
and a Windows 10/11 build path. The compression algorithm itself is
preserved: same input + same parameters produces a byte-identical match
stream to upstream.

The on-disk format extension is \fB.osr\fR (replaces upstream \fB.srep\fR);
old \fB.srep\fR files are not readable by
.B osrep
and vice versa (no backward compatibility by design).

.SH OPTIONS

.SS Compression methods

.TP
.B -m0
In-memory compression (REP algorithm). Works with
.B -d\fIBYTES\fR
to set the dictionary size.
.TP
.B -m1
Fixed-window content-defined chunking; matches verified by VMAC.
.TP
.B -m2
Order-1 content-defined chunking; matches verified by VMAC.
.TP
.B -m3
Match search via VMAC digest comparison. Default if no
.B -m
given. Compression memory ≈ 7-8% of input size.
.TP
.B -m4
Match search via re-reading the input file. Lowest compression memory
(≈ 3-4% of input).
.TP
\fB-m5\fR, \fB-mx\fR
Exhaustive byte-accurate match search. Best compression ratio, slowest.
Compression memory ≈ 7-9% of input.
.TP
\fB-m\fIN\fR\fBf\fR
Future-LZ output: decompression dictionary holds only future matches.
.TP
\fB-m\fIN\fR\fBo\fR
I/O LZ output: output file is used as the decompression dictionary
(decompression memory ≈ 24 MB).

.SS Common options

.TP
\fB-l\fIN\fR
Minimum LZ match length in bytes.
.TP
\fB-c\fIN\fR
Hash chunk size in bytes. Must be 0 (the default) or at least 8: the slice
hash divides by
.IR N /8,
so 1 to 7 would be a division by zero. Those values are refused with exit
status 2 rather than crashing, which is what they used to do.
.TP
\fB-a\fIX\fR[\fB/\fIY\fR]
Compression accelerator: allocate
.I X
bytes per
.I L
input bytes,
.I Y
of which are set. Use
.B -a0
for slowest / least memory.
.TP
\fB-t\fIN\fR
Accepted and \fBnot acted on\fR. The shipped implementation is single-threaded
per block, so there is no worker count to set; the value is parsed and
validated, then discarded. See
.B ACCEPTED AND NOT ACTED ON
below.
.TP
\fB-d\fIBYTES\fR
Dictionary size for
.BR -m0 .
Default 512 MB. Suffixes: k, m, g, t.
.TP
\fB-b\fIBYTES\fR
Compression block size. Default 8 MB.
.TP
\fB-mem\fIBYTES\fR
RAM cap for Future-LZ / Index-LZ decompression. Examples:
.BR -mem75% ,
.BR -mem600mb .
Excess goes to a virtual-memory file.
.TP
\fB-hash=\fIHASH\fR
Per-block hash. One of
.IR vmac ,
.IR siphash ,
.IR md5 ,
.IR sha1 ,
.IR sha512 .
Default
.IR vmac .
.TP
.B -hash-
Disable per-block checksums.
.TP
.BR -mmap ,
.BR -nommap
Force / disable memory-mapped I/O for match checking.
.TP
\fB-temp=\fIPATH\fR
Tempfile path override. Default uses
.B $TMPDIR
on POSIX,
.B %TEMP%
on Windows.
.TP
\fB-vmfile=\fIPATH\fR
Virtual-memory tempfile path.
.TP
\fB-vmblock=\fIBYTES\fR
Block size of the VM tempfile. Default 8 MB.
.TP
\fB-index=\fIFILE\fR
Read/write the per-block match lists in a separate file instead of inside the
archive. The archive keeps its block headers, literals, block-size table and
footer, so it stays self-describing \(em only the lists move. The same
.I FILE
must be given again to decompress.
.IP
Requires
.BI -m N f
or
.BI -m N o\fR.
The default container (Index-LZ) finds its match lists by seeking inside the
archive and never reads the index, so writing one that way produced an archive
that could not be decompressed; the combination is now refused with exit
status 2 instead.
.TP
\fB-s\fIBYTES\fR
Explicitly specify input size (for stdin input). Default 25 GB.
.TP
.B -delete
Delete source file after successful (de)compression.
.TP
\fB-v[0..2]\fR
Verbosity level. Default 2.
.TP
\fB-rem\fIANYTHING\fR
Command-line remark; ignored. Useful in scripts.

.SS Modes

.TP
.B -d
Decompression. With
.BR -m0o ..\c
.BR -m5o ,
needs only ~24 MB beyond OS I/O buffers.
.TP
.B -i
Print archive metadata; do not decompress.

.SS Omega-only additions (F5 / F6)

.TP
.B -dup
Enable FA-style content-defined-chunking dedup pre-pass before SREP.
Long-range duplicates are stripped to a small chunk-table footer
.RB ( ODUP ); SREP only ever processes the unique-chunk body. Streaming
end-to-end so peak RAM is independent of input size. Auto-detected on
decompression. Incompatible with
.BR -m0 ;
recommend
.B -m3
/
.B -m4
/
.BR -m5 .
.TP
.B --dup-paranoid
Byte-compare each dedup hit against the previously-written unique chunk.
Eliminates the 64-bit hash collision window (≈ 1e-7 per million chunks)
at the cost of one disk seek per hit. RAM overhead negligible.
.TP
\fB--chunk-avg=\fIN\fR
CDC average chunk size in bytes. Default 4096.
.TP
\fB--chunk-min=\fIN\fR
CDC minimum chunk size. Default 1024.
.TP
\fB--chunk-max=\fIN\fR
CDC maximum chunk size. Default 16384.
.TP
\fB--chunk-buf=\fIN\fR
Buffer-bounded CDC: rolling hash resets every
.I N
bytes. Default 8 MB. Required for cross-block dedup of large identical
regions.
.TP
\fB--seed=\fIN\fR
Deterministic hash seed (uint64; decimal or
.B 0x
hex). Same seed + same input produces a byte-identical archive across
runs. Default behavior is random per-run (matches upstream SREP
exactly). Useful for content-addressed storage and reproducible builds.
.TP
\fB--format=\fR\fBv4\fR|\fBv5\fR
Container to write. Default
.BR v5 .
Both are always readable regardless of this option; it only selects what
.B osrep
.I writes\fR.
.IP
.B v5
is the current container: a single
.B OSR5
magic, varint records, CRC-32C integrity, and
.B -dup
metadata located through a footer instead of a trailer.
.B v4
is the container the 1.0.x releases read, kept permanently as the
interoperability escape hatch \(em use it when the archive has to be opened
by an older
.B osrep
or by upstream SREP.
.IP
One measured difference if you are choosing between them: with
.BR -dup ,
the v4 metadata carries no checksum, so a corruption inside it can pass the
magic and size checks and yield a silently truncated file with exit status 0.
v5 covers that metadata with a CRC-32C and rejects it. This is a property of
the v4 layout, not a new risk \(em the 1.0.x releases behave identically. See
.I docs/format-spec-v5.md
for the measurement.
.IP
Changing the default from
.B v4
to
.B v5
is the breaking change in 2.0.0. A 1.0.x binary handed a v5 archive does not
misread it: it exits 4 with
.RB \(lq "Not an Omega SREP compressed file (.osr)" \(rq
and writes nothing.

.SS Checking

.TP
.B --verify
Check an archive without reconstructing it, and exit 0 if it is sound.
.IP
This is the one thing the v5 container can do that v1\(en4 cannot. It
validates the header, footer and
.B -dup
metadata CRC\-32Cs, the magic numbers and flag bits, the two block counts
agreeing, every match record against the same range rules the decoder applies,
and that the file ends exactly where the footer says. Truncation, trailing
junk and framing damage are all caught, and it costs one read of the archive.
.IP
It does \fBnot\fR cover the stored block bytes: nothing in v5 checksums a
literal run, so a bit flipped there survives
.B --verify
and is only caught by
.BR -d ,
where the per-block digests verify the reconstruction. The output says so
rather than implying otherwise.
.IP
Because the cost scales with the \fIarchive\fR and a decompress scales with
the \fIoriginal\fR, the gap grows with the compression ratio: measured 5x
faster on a barely-compressible 733 MiB archive, and 180x on one of the same
size holding 5.75 GiB of deduplicated backup.
.IP
A v1\(en4 archive is refused with exit status 2: those containers carry no
checksum anywhere, so the only way to check one is to decompress it.

.SS Help / version

.TP
.BR --help ", " -h ", " -?
Print synopsis and exit.
.TP
.BR --version ", " -V
Print version line and exit.

.SH ACCEPTED AND NOT ACTED ON

These options are parsed and validated, and then have no effect. They exist so
that a command line written against the 1.0.x C++ binary keeps working
unchanged against the Rust implementation that has shipped since 2.0.0.

.TP
\fB-t\fIN\fR
Worker thread count in the C++. The Rust implementation is single-threaded per
block. This is worth knowing beyond the bookkeeping: it is the option most
people reach for when compression feels slow, and here it will not help.
.TP
\fB-a\fIX\fR[\fB/\fIY\fR]
Accelerator sizing. The C++ allocates \fIX\fR bytes per \fIL\fR input bytes
for its bit-array; the Rust implementation allocates nothing for it.
.TP
.BR -mmap ", " -nommap
Memory-mapped reads.
.TP
.BR -ia- ", " -ia+
I/O acceleration.
.TP
.BR -slp ", " -slp- ", " -slp+
Large-page mode.
.TP
\fB-pc\fR[\fIN\fR]
Progress counters, a diagnostic.
.PP
None of these changes the bytes of an archive, and that is measured rather
than assumed: the phase 4c pre-port experiments compared output across
\fB-t1\fR/\fB-t8\fR/default and across \fB-a0\fR/\fB-a16\fR and found it
byte-identical (see \fIdocs/rust-port.md\fR). What they can change in the C++
is speed and memory, which is why they are documented here as inert rather
than removed: silently dropping them would break scripts, and silently
honouring the syntax while implying an effect would be worse.

.B -rem
is different and is listed with the ordinary options: it is a comment, ignored
by both implementations by design.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Generic warning (e.g., partial decompression).
.TP
.B 2
Command-line / option parsing error.
.TP
.B 3
I/O error.
.TP
.B 4
Compression / decompression / archive-format error.
.TP
.B 5
Out of memory.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Tempfile directory on POSIX (also honored:
.BR TMP ,
.BR TEMP ).
.TP
.B TEMP
Tempfile directory on Windows.

.SH FILES
.TP
.B *.osr
Omega SREP archive. On-disk format documented in
.IR docs/format-spec.md .
.TP
.B *.srep
Upstream SREP archive (NOT readable by
.BR osrep ;
use upstream
.B srep
binary instead).

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Compress a tarball:
.PP
.RS 4
.B osrep -m4 backup.tar backup.osr
.RE
.PP
Decompress (auto-detects ODUP trailer if present):
.PP
.RS 4
.B osrep -d backup.osr backup.tar
.RE
.PP
Compress with the Omega F5 dedup pre-pass for long-range duplicate
workloads (backups, installer images):
.PP
.RS 4
.B osrep -dup -m4 backup.tar backup.osr
.RE
.PP
Reproducible archive (same seed → byte-identical output across runs):
.PP
.RS 4
.B osrep --seed=42 -m4 input.bin output.osr
.RE
.PP
FreeArc-style pipeline with ZPAQ as secondary entropy coder:
.PP
.RS 4
.B osrep -m5f -a0 data.tar - | zpaq a final.zpaq -
.RE

.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Per-run hash seed makes archive bytes non-reproducible by default. Use
.B --seed=\fIN\fR
for byte-stable output.
.IP \(bu 2
Targets 32-bit and 64-bit x86 (Linux + Windows 10/11). 64-bit is the
primary, tested target; 32-bit (i686) is supported as an opt-in build.
Big-endian and strict-alignment ports are explicitly out of scope.
.IP \(bu 2
.B -dup
peak RAM scales with the chunk table (~23 KiB per MiB of input at
default chunk parameters); for petabyte-scale inputs the chunk table
itself becomes a soft limit.

